// ===========================================================================
// nightshade/src/render/world_render.hml — THE FRAME GRAPH        (task W3-0a)
//                                          SPLIT INTO MODULES (renderer-split)
// ===========================================================================
//
// ARCHITECTURE.md §5.6: "the frame-graph orchestrator: owns the four batches,
// the RenderEnv, the view/MVP/frustum, the sky mapping and the render-world
// registry; runs stages 8-23 (`frame_render`) and 25-28 (`frame_present`) of §4
// in order. THE ONLY FILE THAT KNOWS THE LAYER ORDER."
//
// This file was EXTRACTED, unchanged in behaviour, out of `tools/shot.hml` in
// W3-0a, which built it in Wave 2 because `src/render/` did not exist yet.
// It has since acquired FOUR consumers — the screenshot harness, the
// interactive walkaround, the frame benchmark and the game itself — and
// `tools/ci_imports.sh` R7 fails the build the moment a tool carries a second
// copy of it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SECOND EXTRACTION: THE PER-STAGE MODULES OF §5.6
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// W3-0a moved the frame graph out of the harness but left every STAGE inside
// it, so this file was 1646 lines and owned the ring walk, the LOD switch, the
// particle shapes and the vignette as well as the order they happen in. §5.6
// names five separate modules for that work and none of them existed. They do
// now, and this file is the orchestrator it was always specified to be:
//
//     terrain_render.hml   stage 13   ring walk, chunk registry, terrain_h
//     entity_render.hml    stage 14   prop registry, LOD, contact blob
//     fx.hml               stage 16   particle registry, 3 shapes, glow cards
//     postfx_geo.hml       stage 23   vignette ring, lightning flash
//     hud.hml              LAYER_HUD  atlas handle, HUD_SOLID texel, primitives
//
// WHY IT WAS WORTH DOING, in this project's own history: Wave 2 shipped a
// visual harness carrying ~650 lines of stand-in art, so every committed
// screenshot depicted art the game never drew and every probe passed. Then
// `shot.hml` carried a private copy of the frame graph until W3-0a extracted it.
// Both failures are the same shape — A MONOLITH THAT THE TOOLS REACH INTO — and
// this was the last instance of it. It also unblocks parallel work: before the
// split, every future renderer task collided on this one file.
//
// The refactor's proof is that NOTHING CHANGED. All nine `tools/shot.hml` scenes
// are byte-identical to their committed `docs/shots/*.png`, and the golden test
// was falsified (one constant mutated, hashes moved, reverted) rather than
// merely observed to be green. Numbers are in the task report.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS FILE STILL OWNS, AND WHAT IT REFUSES TO OWN
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OWNS: the four batches and their caps, the RenderEnv, the view basis and the
// MVP, the frustum, the sky panorama mapping, the viewmodel placement, THE
// LAYER ORDER, the raster order, the readback and the present. The stage
// numbers in the code are §4's. Every stage that is a WALK over a registry now
// lives in the module §5.6 names for it; what stays here is the ORDER.
//
// The public surface is UNCHANGED — the four consumers import ~48 names from
// this file and not one of them moved. Where a name now belongs to a sibling
// module, this file forwards to it (see THE FORWARDING SURFACE at the bottom).
// That is deliberate: a refactor that also rewrites four callers cannot be
// proven byte-identical, because two things changed at once.
//
// REFUSES: colour, time of day, weather, world generation, the HUD's CONTENT,
// the CLI. Two hard reasons, not taste:
//
//   1. THE IMPORT WALL (CLAUDE.md §3, ci_imports.sh R1). `src/render/**` may
//      import wobbleweed, `src/core/**`, `src/render/**`, `src/sim/**` and
//      `@stdlib/*` — and NOT `src/art/**`. So this file cannot name a palette
//      colour, cannot call `tod_eval`, cannot ask `skygen` to re-bake and
//      cannot look up a `meshgen` asset. Everything art-derived arrives as a
//      HANDLE or a NUMBER from the caller: texture handles, mesh handles, atlas
//      cell ids, rgb triples. That is a feature — it is what makes the frame
//      graph testable without an art pipeline, and it is why stages 6
//      (tod_eval) and 7 (skygen re-bake) are the CALLER's, exactly as §4 has
//      them (they sit in the SNAPSHOT block, above VIEW).
//
//   2. The HUD is per-consumer content: the harness draws scene labels, the
//      walkaround draws a frame-budget readout, the game draws the §9 HUD. The
//      HUD must still be emitted AFTER the vignette and BEFORE the flush,
//      because LAYER_HUD is drawn in insertion order — so the frame graph is
//      two calls with a hole in the middle:
//
//          frame_begin()                     stats + counters
//          ... caller: stage 6 tod, stage 7 skygen ...
//          frame_render()                    stages 8..23  (ends with postfx)
//          ... caller: stage 24, its own HUD, into frame_batch_hud() ...
//          frame_present()                   stages 25..28 + readback
//
//      That hole is the ONLY structural difference from the Wave-2 function.
//      Insertion order inside LAYER_HUD is identical, so the pixels are.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DRIVING IT FROM A SECOND CONSUMER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Every per-frame input is a setter, so an interactive client re-drives the
// same frame graph without owning any of it:
//
//     frame_set_camera(x, y, z, yaw, pitch, fov)     every frame
//     frame_set_clear(zenith_r, zenith_g, zenith_b)  every frame
//     frame_set_options(sheet, viewmodel, lightning) every frame
//     tod_eval(frame_env(), day_t, weather)          the caller's stage 6
//
// The world the graph walks is a registry the caller fills — `world_clear()`
// then `world_chunk_add` / `world_prop_add` / `world_fx_add` — so a walkaround
// can rebuild a chunk ring as the player moves without this file knowing what
// a chunk is made of. Those three now forward to the three registries.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE THING HERE THAT IS ON LOAN — AND IT MOVED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `terrain_h` (and the two noise helpers under it) is a WORLD GENERATOR, and it
// now lives in `terrain_render.hml` beside the ring walk that needs it. It is
// still on loan for the same reason: stage 16 lays a ground glow card under
// every dynamic light and the card's corners move with the light, so
// `src/render/fx.hml` imports it too. W3-1 (`src/sim/worldgen.hml`) and W3-2
// (`src/sim/chunk.hml`) own this properly. It is forwarded from here so the
// harness, the walkaround and the game keep the import they already have.
// ===========================================================================

import { sin, cos, sqrt, floor, atan2, asin, divi } from "@stdlib/math";

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the engine
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
